+### Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling
+The parent category covering methods that model 4D radiance fields. KFD-NeRF is chosen when explicit motion modeling via Kalman filtering is preferred over direct time-conditioned feature interpolation or rigid body assumptions. See [Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling](15546-dynamic-scene-and-temporal-modeling.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is comparable to other dynamic NeRF methods. On synthetic data, KFD-NeRF-L takes 3 hours, while KFD-NeRF-S takes 30 minutes [S1]. This is faster than D-NeRF (48 hours) but slower than some voxel-based methods like TiNeuVox-B (40 minutes) [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The tri-plane representation significantly reduces memory consumption compared to MLP-based spatial representations (e.g., D-NeRF uses 16 MB, KFD-NeRF uses 175 MB for the large variant) [S1].
-*   **Performance (Synthetic):** PSNR 35.73 dB, SSIM 0.984, LPIPS 0.045, Average 0.0131 [S1].
-*   **Performance (Real):** PSNR 28.75 dB, SSIM 0.891, LPIPS 0.115, Average 0.0389 [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Accurate deformation estimation from scene observations and predictions; efficient tri-plane representation allows for a shallower observation MLP; smooth deformation fields are easier to fit with MLPs than high-frequency feature interpolation signals [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on local linear motion assumption, which may struggle with highly non-linear or abrupt motions not captured by the velocity approximation [S1]. Requires chronological training order to ensure previous frames offer ample priors [S1].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The tri-plane representation allows for rapid convergence and significantly reduces memory consumption compared to MLP-based spatial representations [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:**
+    *   **Synthetic Data:** PSNR 35.73 dB, SSIM 0.984, LPIPS 0.045, Average 0.0131 [S1].
+    *   **Real Data:** PSNR 28.75 dB, SSIM 0.891, LPIPS 0.115, Average 0.0389 [S1].
+*   **Training Time:** 30 minutes for KFD-NeRF-S and 3 hours for KFD-NeRF-L on the tested datasets, compared to 48 hours for D-NeRF [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Deformation fields are smoother and easier to fit with MLPs due to inductive smoothness bias compared to feature interpolation which exhibits high-frequency signals during abrupt radiance changes [S1]. The Kalman filter automatically balances leveraging predictions in early stages and observations in later stages [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Deformation fields may fail to find correspondences in frames with significant motion if not properly guided [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-quality motion reconstruction is required and computational resources allow for the training of the Kalman filter guided deformation field [S1].
 
 ## Background
-KFD-NeRF was introduced by Y. Zhan, Z. Li, M. Niu, Z. Zhong, S. Nobuhara, K. Nishino, and Y. Zheng from The University of Tokyo, Kyoto University, and Shanghai Artificial Intelligence Laboratory [S1]. It was published in 2024 (arXiv:2407.13185v1, July 18, 2024) [S1]. The method draws inspiration from control theory, specifically the Kalman filter, to model the 4D radiance field as a dynamic system [S1]. It addresses the limitations of previous dynamic NeRF methods that either disregard motion priors (e.g., DyNeRF, KPlanes) or rely on rigid deformation fields that fail to capture non-rigid motion [S1].
+KFD-NeRF was introduced by Li, Niu, Zhong, Nobuhara, Nishino, and Zheng in 2024 [S1]. It draws inspiration from control theory, modeling the 4D radiance field as a dynamic system with temporally-varying states [S1]. The method integrates a plug-and-play Kalman filter module to optimize state estimations from both observations and predictions [S1]. It builds on the observation that deformation fields have an inductive smoothness bias suitable for MLPs, unlike feature interpolation [S1].
 
